  "subtitle": "A desktop optical-mark reader that turns a human-filled bubble sheet into machine-readable data — paper as the answer-and-data token.",
  "why_scout_cares": "The interaction model IS the point: the human answers questions by darkening ovals on a printed paper form, and the machine reads those fills by sweeping light and measuring contrasting reflectivity. The bubble sheet is genuinely an input token — a hand-marked, machine-readable data entry medium requiring no keyboard and no typed characters. This is the museum's first form-based (OMR) data entry artifact; bubbled-paper as computer input is unrepresented across all 295 existing exhibits. Distinct from OCR (Caere OCR Wand reads pre-printed characters; Scantron reads the human's own marks), and from the supermarket laser scanner (reads a machine-printed barcode).",
